From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mb/lenovo/haswell: Print the TSC and the xHCI ports along the
 boot

Test builds of the W541 only, not for a release: prints, no change of
behaviour, for two open items of docs/roadmap.md.

Linux logs "[Firmware Bug]: TSC ADJUST differs: CPU0 0 --> N" at every
resume from S3, with N near the TSC of the suspend. Nothing in coreboot
writes the TSC or IA32_TSC_ADJUST, so the prints say where the value
appears: the TSC and TSC_ADJUST of the boot processor at the start and
at the end of romstage, at BS_PRE_DEVICE, around BS_DEV_INIT (the MP
init and the SMM relocation), at BS_POST_DEVICE, at BS_OS_RESUME, in
mainboard_suspend_resume() right before the jump to the wake vector, at
BS_PAYLOAD_BOOT on the boot path, and of every processor at the start
of cpu_core_init(), before and after the MP init on the boot one.

A USB 3.0 drive in a SuperSpeed port links at a cold boot and across a
suspend, and stays in Polling with the reset bit set, or in Compliance
mode, after every warm reset on the nri variant. The prints follow the
ports: PORTSC of the SuperSpeed ports and the routing registers
(USB2PR, USB3PR, their masks, USB3FUS, the port disable registers,
PMIR) at the start of romstage, at the entry of the switching flow of
the early USB init, before and after the WPR of warm_reset_usb3_ports()
and at its end, with the routing set, at the exit of early_usb_init()
and 10 ms later, then in ramstage at the entry and exit of
usb_xhci_init(), in usb_xhci_reset_usb3() (the ports as found, which
ones get the warm reset, whether WRC came and when) and at the boot
states above. PM1_STS, PM1_CNT, GEN_PMCON_2 and GEN_PMCON_3 go with
them, to tell a resume from a warm reset in the log.

All at BIOS_NOTICE, so that the Info level of the setup shows them.
The one delay added is the 10 ms at the exit of early_usb_init(). The
T440p shares the mainboard and the processor code; the M920x is not
touched.
